--TEST--
`Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::call` runs a `public` method and refuses a `private` one, with a wrong class and a misspelling told apart from neither — `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::call
// `rule:security/reflection-enforces-visibility`: *acting* on a member runs the check ordinary code at the call
// site would run, and there is no `setAccessible` to lift it with. `call` does
// not restate that rule anywhere — it hands the call to the same path an
// ordinary call through an erased receiver takes — so what this pins is that
// the two are one check rather than two that happen to agree today.
class Vault {
    public string $label = "main";

    public function open(string $who): string {
        return $who . " opened " . $this->label;
    }

    private function combination(): string {
        return "0000";
    }
}

var $vault = new Vault();
var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject($vault);

// The public half runs, and the entries fill the parameters in order.
echo $described->call($vault, "open", ["ana"]), "\n";

// The same call written ordinarily, through a `mixed` receiver — the one
// ordinary call site that is outside every class by construction, which is
// exactly the premise a reflective call site has. Both lines print, so the
// agreement below is not two doors that are simply both shut.
mixed $erased = $vault;
echo $erased->open("ben"), "\n";

// The private half is refused. `LogicError` rather than `RuntimeError`: a call
// site outside the class was never entitled to that method, which is a fact
// about the program rather than about this value.
try {
    var $secret = $described->call($vault, "combination", []);
    echo "private call", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    echo "private refused", "\n";
}

// And ordinarily, from the same erased receiver — the refusal reflection did
// not lift is the refusal an ordinary call already met.
try {
    var $ordinary = $erased->combination();
    echo "private ordinary call", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $also) {
    echo "private refused ordinarily", "\n";
}

// A name that names no method at all is refused too, so a misspelling is never
// mistaken for a member that exists and is closed.
try {
    var $missing = $described->call($vault, "opne", ["cass"]);
    echo "misspelled call", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $wrong) {
    echo "no such method", "\n";
}

// A description is of a *class*, so another class's instance is refused even
// where the name would have resolved on it.
class Impostor {
    public function open(string $who): string {
        return $who . " opened nothing";
    }
}
try {
    var $borrowed = $described->call(new Impostor(), "open", ["dee"]);
    echo "borrowed call", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $mismatch) {
    echo "wrong class", "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
ana opened main
ben opened main
private refused
private refused ordinarily
no such method
wrong class
